{0 Fetcher}

A {!Workero.Fetcher} sends HTTP requests through a service binding, an assets
binding, or a Durable Object stub. Use a service binding to call another
Worker without a public endpoint; use an assets binding to serve files from
your Worker. See the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/service-bindings/}service binding reference}.

{1 Configure}

Declare the target Worker in [wrangler.toml]. [binding] is the name that you pass
to [of_env]:

{@toml[
[[services]]
binding = "BACKEND"
service = "backend-worker"
]}

For an assets binding, set [directory] to the folder of files and [binding] to
the name that {!Workero.Fetcher.of_env} opens:

{@toml[
[assets]
directory = "./public"
binding = "ASSETS"
]}

{1 Call another Worker}

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let backend = Fetcher.of_env env "BACKEND" in
  match URL.path (Request.url req) with
  | "/users" ->
      Fetcher.fetch ~method_:POST
        ~headers:[ ("content-type", "application/json") ]
        ~body:{|{"name":"Ada"}|} backend "http://backend/users"
  | _ -> Fetcher.fetch_request backend req

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- {!Workero.Fetcher.of_env} opens the binding. It raises [Invalid_argument] when
  there is no such binding, or when the binding has no [fetch] method.
- {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch} sends a request and waits for the response headers.
  It takes the same optional arguments as {!Workero.fetch}. A [GET] or [HEAD]
  request with a body raises [Invalid_argument].
- {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch_request} forwards a {!Workero.Request.t} as it is. The
  runtime consumes the body of the request. Use {!Workero.Request.clone} first
  if the handler reads it too.
- {!Workero.Entrypoint.call} calls an RPC method of a binding that names an
  entrypoint class. See {{!page-entrypoints}Entrypoints}.

Use an absolute URL. The service binding selects the target Worker, regardless
of the host in [http://backend/users]. The target's [fetch] handler receives
the request URL. A call through {!Workero.Durable_object.Namespace.get} uses
the same API; see {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.

If the target raises, the call raises {!Workero.Js_error}. A target that answers
with a status such as 404 or 500 is not an error: read the status with
{!Workero.Response.status}.

{1 Serve a static asset}

Put a file at [public/hello.txt], configure the [assets] binding above, and
use the fetcher in a handler:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let assets = Workero.Fetcher.of_env env "ASSETS" in
  Workero.Fetcher.fetch assets "https://assets/hello.txt"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [npx wrangler dev] and request [/]. The response body is the content of
[public/hello.txt]. The assets binding is simulated locally. On deploy,
[npx wrangler deploy] uploads the directory in [assets]; there is no separate
asset resource to create. See
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/binding/}Static Assets binding}.

{1 Verify the service binding}

Run the target Worker in the same [wrangler dev] session by passing several
configuration files:

{@sh[
npx wrangler dev -c wrangler.toml -c ../backend/wrangler.toml
]}

Request [/users] locally and check that the backend receives the [POST] and
JSON body. Deploy both Workers before testing the service call on Cloudflare.
The workero tests exercise a service binding in [workerd]; they do not test
the assets binding. See
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/service-bindings/}Cloudflare service bindings}.
